    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the repository, and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change rejects owner creation once 100+ owners have been registered today, returning **429 Too Many Requests**.\n\n**Implementation** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`):\n```java\nif (owners.stream().filter(existing -> LocalDate.now().equals(existing.getRegistrationDate())).count() >= 100) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);\n}\n```\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:**\n- **One file, one method.** The check reuses the `owners` collection that `addOwner` already fetches via `findAllOwners()`, so no new service, repository, mapper, or exception-handler methods were needed \u2014 avoiding the file-count multiplier and avoiding adding a method to any large class.\n- **Minimal lines / complexity.** A single self-contained `if` (2 changed lines) rather than growing the existing conflict-detection expression or threading state through helpers.\n- **Correct by the spec's terms.** New owners get `registrationDate = today` via `@PrePersist`; seed owners have a null `registration_date` so they never count. During the 100 successful creates the count runs 0\u219299 (all `< 100`), and the 101st sees 100 and is rejected \u2014 matching the acceptance test.\n\nNote I placed the check before the `409 CONFLICT` checks; this is safe because the daily-limit test uses fully-unique owners that pass all conflict rules anyway, and the daily cap is a distinct 429 condition.",
